LightWebPres Output Exception, version 1.0

Additional permission under section 7 of the GNU General Public License,
version 3.

When lightwebpres sets up, refreshes, or builds a series, it writes files
that contain material copied from lightwebpres itself: template scaffolds
such as templates/settings.conf and templates/custom.css, scripts such as
templates/nav.js, language packs, configuration files, demonstration
articles, and the HTML, CSS and JavaScript of every page it builds.

In this exception, "Covered Output" means every file that lightwebpres
writes or generates, and every portion of lightwebpres embedded in such a
file -- but not any complete copy of the lightwebpres program itself, such
as the copy of this executable that `lightwebpres init` places in the
series directory. That copy remains governed by the GNU General Public
License, and if you distribute it -- for instance by publishing your series
repository -- the License's conditions apply to it, including passing on a
copy of the License.

As a special exception, you have permission to copy, modify, and convey
Covered Output, and any work containing it -- a published presentation, for
example -- under terms of your choice, free of the conditions the GNU
General Public License would otherwise place on it, so long as the work
you convey under this permission is not itself a document or presentation
generator that uses Covered Output, or a modified version of it, as its
own templates or scaffolds. (You may still convey such a generator; you
simply convey it under the GNU General Public License, without this
special permission.)

Portions of Covered Output that come from third parties remain under their
own license terms; the copyright and license notices that lightwebpres
writes into its files must be kept with them. See THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.

This exception grants nothing beyond the GNU General Public License for
lightwebpres itself. As provided by section 7, anyone who conveys a copy
of lightwebpres, modified or not, may remove this exception from that
copy; output produced by a copy from which it has been removed is governed
by the GNU General Public License without this exception.
